<!-- Website teaching layer for android/kotlin-multiplatform/ecosystem/dependency-injection.md. Original preserved in full. Structure defined in android-phase9-structure.md before drafting. Checked 2026-10-25 against https://kotlinlang.org/docs/api-guidelines-build-for-multiplatform.html. -->

<!-- @opening -->
<p class="story-lead">The shared repository is portable, but constructing it needs an Android database driver in one app and an Apple driver in the other. Dependency injection is the act of supplying those collaborators. Choosing a container is a separate decision.</p>

Start at each application’s composition root and follow construction, ownership and cleanup.

<!-- @after 1. Put the Composition Boundary at the Platform Edge :: visual -->
### Construction belongs at the edge

<figure class="viz"><div class="viz-grid" style="--cols:2"><div class="node"><strong>Common constructor</strong><small>Declares the dependencies the behaviour needs</small></div><div class="node"><strong>Platform root</strong><small>Supplies engines, paths, drivers and native services</small></div><div class="node"><strong>Graph mechanism</strong><small>Manual calls or a deliberately selected DI tool</small></div><div class="node"><strong>Resource owner</strong><small>Defines lifetime, scope closure and release</small></div></div><figcaption>A singleton registration describes instance reuse; it does not by itself define every resource’s cleanup or coroutine lifetime.</figcaption></figure>

<!-- @after 4. Manual DI Is a First-Class Option :: animation -->
### Build and close one owned graph

<figure class="stepper"><span class="stepper-title">Build and close one owned graph</span><div class="stage viz-grid" style="--cols:2"><div class="node" data-el="n0"><strong>Platform root</strong><small data-text="v0">—</small></div><div class="node" data-el="n1"><strong>Resources</strong><small data-text="v1">—</small></div><div class="node" data-el="n2"><strong>Shared service</strong><small data-text="v2">—</small></div><div class="node" data-el="n3"><strong>Lifetime</strong><small data-text="v3">—</small></div></div><script type="application/json" class="steps">[{"label": "Configure", "caption": "The application chooses platform inputs and configuration. Tests can supply different collaborators through the same constructors.", "add": ["n0", "n1", "n2", "n3"], "state": {"n0": "active", "n1": "idle", "n2": "idle", "n3": "idle"}, "text": {"v0": "Owns setup", "v1": "Not created", "v2": "Not created", "v3": "Explicit owner"}}, {"label": "Construct", "caption": "Create the engine/driver and pass them into the shared graph. The example does not require a global service locator.", "add": ["n0", "n1", "n2", "n3"], "state": {"n0": "idle", "n1": "active", "n2": "idle", "n3": "idle"}, "text": {"v0": "Supplies dependencies", "v1": "Target-owned handles", "v2": "Receives collaborators", "v3": "Ownership documented"}}, {"label": "Use", "caption": "The shared service uses its declared dependencies. Manual wiring checks constructor types; runtime resource behaviour still needs tests.", "add": ["n0", "n1", "n2", "n3"], "state": {"n0": "idle", "n1": "idle", "n2": "active", "n3": "idle"}, "text": {"v0": "No hidden lookup", "v1": "Alive while needed", "v2": "Domain operation", "v3": "Active"}}, {"label": "Close", "caption": "At the documented graph lifetime boundary, close resources according to their APIs and ownership order. Do not rely on a UI re-render as teardown.", "add": ["n0", "n1", "n2", "n3"], "state": {"n0": "idle", "n1": "idle", "n2": "idle", "n3": "active"}, "text": {"v0": "Coordinates release", "v1": "Deterministic cleanup", "v2": "No further use", "v3": "Closed and tested"}}]</script><figcaption>Illustrative sequence, not a measured trace. <a href="https://kotlinlang.org/docs/api-guidelines-build-for-multiplatform.html" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Primary contract</a>.</figcaption></figure>

<!-- @after 4. Manual DI Is a First-Class Option :: check -->
### Try the decision yourself

<details><summary>Is Dagger reflective by design?</summary><p>No. Its ordinary graph implementation is generated; its target support is a separate concern.</p></details>
<details><summary>Does a graph compile prove native startup and cleanup?</summary><p>No. Runtime configuration, target composition, resource lifetime and consumer integration need evidence.</p></details>
<details><summary>Must common classes call a container?</summary><p>Constructor injection can keep their dependencies explicit without container access.</p></details>

